  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1155 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nFriday, the departure of the weather system to the northeast \nallows the broad high pressure system across southern CONUS to \nbuild back across the southern plains. This will likely bring a\nsmall stint of hot and dry conditions to the panhandles with\nthe potential for triple digit highs. \n\nCome the weekend the high pressure starts to shift back out of the\nsouthern plains as a deepening large scale weather system pushes\nsoutheast across western CONUS. This action will allow for a few\nsmall scale weather system to pass across the southern plains\nduring the weekend. The passage of these small scale system will\nbring a low (10-30%) chance for rain showers and thunderstorms to\nthe panhandles. The overall quantity of moisture with these system\nis most likely to be low so the amount of rainfall during this\ntime will also most likely be low. Even with the passage of the\nweather system it will most likely remain hot with highs in the\n90s to low 100s. \n\nThe main weather system is most likely to push into the southern\nplains early next work week. This would bring more substantial\nmoisture and instability to the panhandles. This will allow for a\nlow to medium (20-50%) chance for rain showers and thunderstorms\nacross the whole of the panhandles. With the higher moisture more\nrainfall can occur during this time that has the potential for\nmeaningful rain accumulations. The passage of the weather system\nwill have a medium to high chance of bringing cooler temperatures\nto the panhandles.",
